    <pre> <h1>                   Exercise Name: Wide-Grip Pull-Up</h1></pre> 
        <u><b><h4>Exercise Overview</h4></b></u>

This 3D anatomical visualization demonstrates Wide Overhand Pull-Ups (Wide-Grip Pull-Ups) viewed from
     behind. The video highlights the back muscles—specifically the upper lats, middle back, and shoulders
     (Latissimus Dorsi, Rhomboids, Trapezius, and Rear Deltoids)—in glowing orange and blue, illustrating 
     how pulling your body weight with a wide overhand grip fully engages the lats to build upper body strength
      and back width.
<pre>
 <b>Exercise Name:</b> Wide-Grip Pull-Up
 
 <b>Target Muscles:</b> Lats (Latissimus Dorsi — upper outer fibers)

 <b>Secondary Muscles:</b> Rhomboids, Trapezius, Rear Deltoids, Biceps (Biceps Brachii), Forearms, Core
</pre>
</div></div>

<h1>
   
    How to Perform (Step-by-Step)</h1><u><b><h4>1. Setup</h4></b></u>: Reach up and grab a pull-up bar using an overhand grip (palms facing away from you) wider than shoulder-width apart. Hang freely with your arms fully extended and your core engaged.
 <u><b><h4>2. Pulling Phase (Concentric)</h4></b></u>: Exhale as you pull your body upward by driving your elbows down and back toward your sides. Focus on depressing your shoulder blades and pulling your chest toward the bar.
 <u><b><h4>3. Peak Squeeze</h4></b></u>: Pause briefly at the top position when your chin clears the bar, squeezing your lats and upper back muscles together.
 <u><b><h4>4. Lowering Phase (Eccentric)</h4></b></u>: Inhale as you slowly lower your body back down under full control over 2 to 3 seconds until your arms reach full extension at the dead hang position.



<h1>Key Benefits</h1>

 <b>◉V-Taper Physique:</b> Wide overhand grip directly targets the outer latissimus dorsi, giving your back a wider, broader V-shape appearance.<br>
 
 <b>◉Functional Relative Strength:</b> Master's pulling your own body weight, improving overall upper-body strength, grip strength, and shoulder stability.<br>
 
 <b>◉Postural Improvement:</b> Strengthens the deep postural muscles of the upper back, counteracting rounded shoulders and poor desk posture.


<h1>Recommended Sets & Reps</h1>

| Goal | Sets | Reps | Rest |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Muscle Building (Hypertrophy) | 3 – 4 | 8 – 12 | 90 seconds |
| Endurance | 3 – 4 | 12 – 15 (or max bodyweight reps) | 60 seconds |
| Strength | 4 – 5 | 4 – 6 (weighted if needed) | 2 – 3 minutes |

Keep your movement controlled and avoid swinging your legs or using momentum (kipping) to pull yourself up to the bar.

